# Design: the node encoding, version 1. The page spec/node-encoding.md is the authority; these nodes say what it chose and why.

n: 5.13
name: node-encoding
kind: hub
is: Version 1 of the node encoding. A node's canonical bytes are a kind byte, its blueprint's id and either leaf bytes or child ids; its content id is SHA-256 of those bytes. The id of 32 zero bytes is nothing. The catalogue is numbers under one root, with names beside it. The canonical print has four forms and writes a repeated node as a back-reference. A reader refuses whatever is not canonical. Two readers by two hands hold its 142 vectors.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.13.1
name: canonical-bytes
kind: decision
is: A leaf is the byte 00, the 32-byte id of its blueprint and its bytes. A composite is the byte 01, the 32-byte id of its blueprint and one 32-byte id per child in order. There is no length field: the hash covers the whole message and a store frames its own records. Kind bytes 02 to ff are kept for later versions.
status: inferred
from: 5.1.1 5.2.2
src: 2.30.3#2
conf: mid
why: Child ids, not child bytes, keep a node's bytes small whatever it holds and let a reader check one node without its children.
flip: A store that must walk a node without an index of ids would want the children's bytes inline.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.13.2
name: nothing-and-the-root
kind: decision
is: The id of 32 zero bytes names nothing and is the id of no node. It stands in the blueprint position of exactly one node, the root, a leaf with no bytes, and never in a child position. Every other node's blueprint is a node, so the regress of blueprints ends at the root.
status: inferred
from: 1.1 5.1.1
src: 2.30.3#3-4
conf: mid
limit: That no input hashes to 32 zero bytes is the usual assumption about SHA-256, not a proof.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.13.3
name: catalogue-is-numbers
kind: decision
is: Catalogue entry N, from 1 to 4,294,967,295, is the leaf under the root whose bytes are the number N. An entry's content is its number and its name is a lens row beside it, so naming or renaming an entry changes no id. Version 1 names entries 1 to 14 as leaf blueprints and 16 as list, keeps 15 and 17 to 31, and puts no rule on 32 and above, where the core operations will be named.
status: inferred
from: 5.1.5 5.2.4 1.9.2
src: 2.30.3#4
conf: mid
why: The numeric space is the root and a symbol is attached beside it, as the owner's request describes; the numbers are fixed by the page, not by arrival order.
limit: The core operations are not named yet; that belongs to stage 2 with the walker's laws.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.13.4
name: leaf-rules
kind: decision
is: Integers and floats are little-endian. A nat or int is the shortest form and zero is the empty string. Sized integers have exactly their width. A float has one NaN and keeps negative zero apart. Text is well-formed UTF-8 and is not normalised. Each value so has one leaf.
status: inferred
from: 5.1.4 5.7.1
src: 2.30.3#5
conf: mid
why: Both first targets are little-endian and frozen nodes are read where they lie. Normalising text would tie the encoding to a Unicode version.
open: 6.1.11
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.13.5
name: print-with-back-references
kind: decision
is: The canonical print has four forms: '#N' for a catalogue entry, '[blueprint hex]' for a leaf, '(blueprint children)' for a composite, and '^k' for the node opened k-th in the same print. A node is written in full the first time and as '^k' after, so a print grows with the number of distinct nodes and not with the number of paths to them.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.4 3.13.2.11
src: 2.30.3#6
conf: mid
why: The 2026-11-05 carrier wrote children in full and its bytes doubled at every level of a node that names one child twice.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.13.6
name: reader-refuses-what-is-not-canonical
kind: decision
is: A reader accepts only the one canonical form of a node, as bytes and as text, and names the first rule broken with one of seventeen words. It reads text once from left to right, checks a node when its bracket closes, and stops at the first fault it meets. Reading and printing are therefore inverse: a printed node reads back to the same node, and accepted text prints back to the same text.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.4 5.3.8 5.11.4
src: 2.30.3#7
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.13.7
name: a-reader-holds-what-it-accepts
kind: decision
is: A reader that takes nodes in from bytes keeps each one it accepts, and refuses as 'unknown' a node whose blueprint or child is the id of no node it holds. It holds the root and entries 1 to 31 from the start and may hold more; the bootstrap starts with entries 0 to 63. This is the one refusal that depends on what was read before, so the lines of a vector file are read in order.
status: inferred
from: 5.13.1 5.14.7 5.13.8
src: 2.30.3#7
conf: mid
why: A store places a node under a blueprint and over children it has. The word was on no page until two readers were run on the same bytes and one of them said it.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.13.8
name: two-readers-hold-the-vectors
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25 with 'make check': tools/second.c, a reader, printer and store written from the page by a session that had not opened the bootstrap, holds the 142 vectors, as the bootstrap's reader does. Each of the 14 ground programs frozen by either reader thaws in the other to the same id and print, and the two write the same ice bytes.
status: observed
src: 2.30.13 2.30.7 2.30.3
see: 6.3.14 6.1.30
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.13.9
name: two-readers-agree-on-variations
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25 with 'make check' and 'make hands': the second reader writes 200,000 variations of the vectors with its verdict on each, as text and as bytes, and all 200,049 lines hold in both readers. The bootstrap reads them in about 30 ms; a second process recording its region sees the node table grow from 65 records to 8,788, which is its 64 entries, the record for nothing and the 8,723 nodes the second reader holds.
status: observed
src: 2.30.13 2.30.7 2.30.10
limit: The variations are small edits of the vectors from one seed. A planted fault they caught and the vectors then missed: a 32-bit integer taken as eight bytes wide. One they missed: an overlong three-byte text, now a vector.
at: 2026-11-25

